; Sample journal — demonstrates the plain-text accounting format
; supported by SwiftLedger.

; Account declarations are optional. They let you explicitly set the
; account type when the name root doesn't match the recognised conventions.
; An indented line under one is a subdirective of it, and one hledger does
; not know is skipped, here too: the decimal-mark under the first account
; declares nothing, and the dollars below are still read with the point. So
; is a format line, which states a style only under a commodity block: the one
; below leaves the dollars alone, and an entry SwiftLedger rebuilds in dollars
; is still written with the point.
account Assets:Checking
    decimal-mark ,
    format $1.000,00
account Assets:Savings
account Assets:Brokerage
account Assets:PettyCash
account Assets:EuroAccount
account Assets:PoundAccount
account Assets:SwissAccount
account Liabilities:CreditCard
account Equity:Opening
account Income:Salary
account Income:Freelance
account Expenses:Rent
account Expenses:Food:Groceries
account Expenses:Food:Restaurants
account Expenses:Transport
account Expenses:Utilities

; ── January ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

2024-01-01 * Opening balances
    Assets:Checking        $2500.00
    Assets:Savings         $5000.00
    Equity:Opening

2024-01-15 * Salary
    Assets:Checking        $3200.00
    Income:Salary

2024-01-01 * Rent — January
    Expenses:Rent          $1200.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-01-07 Groceries
    Expenses:Food:Groceries    $87.45
    Liabilities:CreditCard

2024-01-12 Lunch with client
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  $42.00
    Liabilities:CreditCard

2024-01-20 Monthly transit pass
    Expenses:Transport         $95.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-01-22 Electricity bill
    Expenses:Utilities         $68.30
    Assets:Checking

2024-01-28 Pay off credit card
    Liabilities:CreditCard     $129.45
    Assets:Checking

; ── February ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

2024-02-01 * Rent — February
    Expenses:Rent          $1200.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-02-05 Supermarket run
    Expenses:Food:Groceries    $112.60
    Liabilities:CreditCard

2024-02-10 Freelance project payment
    Assets:Checking        $800.00
    Income:Freelance

2024-02-15 * Salary
    Assets:Checking        $3200.00
    Income:Salary

2024-02-18 ! Pending refund  ; status: pending
    Assets:Checking        $35.00
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants

2024-02-20 Bus and train fares
    Expenses:Transport         $42.50
    Assets:Checking

2024-02-25 Internet and phone
    Expenses:Utilities         $55.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-02-28 Pay off credit card
    Liabilities:CreditCard     $112.60
    Assets:Checking

; ── Entries with fewer than two postings ────────────────────────────────────
; The rule is that every commodity nets to zero, not that there are two
; postings, so these are valid in ledger, in hledger and here.

; A dated line on its own is how a journal keeps a note.
2024-02-03 Called the bank about the card fee

; A single posting of zero touches an account without moving money.
2024-02-06 Opened a brokerage account
    Assets:Brokerage       $0

; With nothing to balance against, an elided amount reads as zero, exactly
; as if 0 had been written.
2024-02-08 Set up the petty cash tin
    Assets:PettyCash

; The usual hledger shape: a zero posting carrying a balance assertion, which
; states the balance on that date without recording a movement. SwiftLedger
; loads the file whether or not the assertion holds, and checks it as hledger
; does when asked.
2024-02-29 Statement check
    Assets:Savings         $0 = $5000.00

; A doubled = asserts that the commodity is all the account holds: these
; savings are dollars and nothing else. =* and ==* would count the
; subaccounts' postings too.
2024-03-01 Statement check, dollars only
    Assets:Savings         $0 == $5000.00

; ── Dates written other ways ────────────────────────────────────────────────
; hledger writes a date with '-', '/' or '.' between its parts, the month and
; the day with one digit or two, and a secondary date may leave out its year,
; which it then takes from the primary date. The = stands directly between
; the two dates, with no space on either side: after a space it opens the
; description instead, and a space after it refuses the line, as in hledger.
; All three entries are read as hledger reads them, and an edited one is
; written back as YYYY-MM-DD.

2024/3/4 Taxi from the station
    Expenses:Transport         $18.00
    Assets:Checking

2024.03.06 Bus ticket
    Expenses:Transport         $2.50
    Assets:Checking

2024-03-07=03-08 Train ticket, charged the next day
    Expenses:Transport         $24.00
    Assets:Checking

; ── A posting with a date of its own ────────────────────────────────────────
; A date: tag in a posting's comment dates that posting on its own, as hledger
; reads it, and so does a date in square brackets. The water bill is written
; on the day it arrived and leaves the checking account on the day it cleared,
; so a balance as of the end of March still counts it as unpaid. The comment
; is kept as written, and the entry keeps its own date.

2024-03-29 Water bill, cleared on the 2nd
    Expenses:Utilities         $31.00
    Assets:Checking  ; date:2024-04-02

; An elided line that absorbs two commodities is read as a posting in each,
; and both take the line's date, though only one holds the comment. An entry
; SwiftLedger rebuilds writes them on lines of their own, and the one without
; the comment gets a date: tag, so the next load dates it the same.

2024-03-30 Ferry tickets on the card, cleared on the 3rd
    Expenses:Transport     €12,00
    Expenses:Transport     £9.00
    Liabilities:TravelCard  ; date:2024-04-03

; ── Virtual postings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Parentheses make a posting virtual: it moves money in a tracking account
; outside the double-entry books and takes no part in balancing. Square
; brackets make it balanced virtual — exempt from balancing against the real
; postings, but the bracketed postings of one entry must sum to zero among
; themselves. Both names are stored bare, so a query finds the same account
; however the journal spells it.

2024-03-01 Groceries, and move the food envelope
    Expenses:Food:Groceries             $60.00
    Assets:Checking
    [Assets:Checking:Envelope:Food]     -$60.00
    [Assets:Checking:Available]         $60.00
    (Reserve:Capital)                   $250.00

; ── A total price with a sign of its own ────────────────────────────────────
; A sign written on a total price is kept, and the sign of the amount it
; prices is applied on top of it, as hledger weighs it: €100,00 @@ -$110.00
; costs -$110.00, so the $110.00 below balances it and -$110.00 would not.

2024-03-02 Euros booked against a negative total
    Assets:EuroAccount     €100,00 @@ -$110.00
    Assets:Checking        $110.00

; ── Several commodities ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Each commodity balances on its own, and one elided posting absorbs the
; remainder of every commodity in the entry, which is how ledger and hledger
; read it. The equity line below is read as two postings, -£400.00 and
; -€1.500,00, sorted by symbol as hledger sorts them, though the file keeps
; the one line as written. The euro account is written the way a European
; statement writes it, with a comma dividing the fraction and a point grouping
; the thousands.

2024-03-05 * Opening balances for the accounts abroad
    Assets:EuroAccount     €1.500,00
    Assets:PoundAccount    £400.00
    Equity:Opening

; A commodity the written postings already net to zero leaves nothing to
; absorb, so the elided line gets no posting in it: here only -£25.00.
2024-03-09 Lunch and a train abroad
    Expenses:Transport     €30,00
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  £25.00
    Assets:EuroAccount     -€30,00
    Assets:PoundAccount

; A code can stand in front of its number too, with a space between them:
; CHF 80.00 is eighty francs, the same commodity as CHF80.00, as it is in
; ledger and in hledger. The space is layout and no part of the name, and an
; entry SwiftLedger rebuilds in francs gets it back because this file writes it.
2024-03-10 Dinner in Zurich
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  CHF 80.00
    Assets:SwissAccount    -CHF 80.00

; A sign can stand apart from its number, and a commodity can follow the
; number as readily as lead it: - 4 VTI is four shares out, as it is in ledger
; and in hledger. The space after the sign is layout, like the one before VTI.
; The sign itself is written once: - - 4 VTI is no amount, and neither is a
; bare - - 4, which hledger refuses as it refuses the first.
2024-03-11 Shares moved to another broker
    Assets:Brokerage:Old   - 4 VTI
    Assets:Brokerage:New   4 VTI

; ── An elided line with nothing left to absorb ──────────────────────────────
; When the written postings already net to zero in every commodity, the
; elided line still takes a zero in each of them, sorted by symbol, as hledger
; reads it: the restaurants line below is £0.00 and €0,00. It is only when
; some commodity leaves something over that the ones netting out are dropped.

2024-03-11 Card charges abroad reversed by the bank
    Assets:EuroAccount     -€8,00
    Assets:EuroAccount     €8,00
    Assets:PoundAccount    -£6.00
    Assets:PoundAccount    £6.00
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants

; ── Decimal marks and a comment after an amount ─────────────────────────────
; Which mark divides the fraction is read out of the number: a mark with
; anything other than three digits after it divides, so €12,50 is twelve euros
; fifty, as most of Europe writes it, and the €1.500,00 in the opening entry
; above is fifteen hundred. hledger and ledger 3 both read €12,50 that way, so
; the entry below means the same in either tool. A mark with exactly three
; digits after it divides as well, as hledger reads it, so the fuel below is
; thirty-eight and a quarter litres at one euro eighty; ledger-cli would read
; 38,250 as thirty-eight thousand, and a file written for it says which it
; means with a commodity or decimal-mark line, as the invoice further down
; does. An entry SwiftLedger rebuilds is written back with the marks the file
; uses for that commodity, and this file uses both: an edited euro entry comes
; back €12,50 while an edited dollar entry comes back $1200.00, each in the
; style its own amounts are written in.
; A ; after an amount opens the posting's comment even with one space in front
; of it, as it does in ledger and in hledger, since two spaces are what end an
; account name and the name has ended by then.

2024-03-12 Coffee in Vienna
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  €12,50 ; one space is enough to start a comment
    Assets:EuroAccount     -€12,50

2024-03-13 Fuel, metered to the millilitre
    Expenses:Car           38,250 L @ €1,80
    Assets:EuroAccount

; ── A directive settles the one shape a number cannot ───────────────────────
; €1.000 is a thousand grouped or one euro to three places, and nothing in the
; number says which. Without a directive it is one euro, as hledger reads it.
; A commodity directive states the marks, and from the line it stands on
; every such amount in that commodity is read with the declared decimal mark,
; so the invoice below is a thousand euros, as hledger reads it. A D directive
; does the same for every commodity without a declaration of its own, and
; hledger's decimal-mark directive for all of them at once. The directive
; line itself is kept verbatim, like every other directive.

commodity €1.000,00

2024-03-15 Freelance invoice paid in euros
    Assets:EuroAccount     €1.000
    Income:Freelance

; ── An amount written against the declaration ──────────────────────────────
; From the directive above on, every lone mark in a euro amount is read by it
; rather than by the number: the comma divides, and a point between digits
; groups whatever follows it, as hledger reads them; only a point with nothing
; on one side of it, as in €5. or €.50, still divides, since there is nothing
; there to group. So €45.00 below is forty-five hundred
; euros, not forty-five. An amount written that way is most likely a slip,
; and reading it as hledger does means SwiftLedger reports the figure hledger
; reports rather than one a hundred times smaller, with nothing in the file
; to show the two disagree. The line is kept as written, like every entry
; SwiftLedger does not rebuild.

2024-03-16 Winter tyres, typed with the wrong mark
    Expenses:Car           €45.00
    Assets:EuroAccount

; ── A line of nothing but spaces ────────────────────────────────────────────
; The line under the entry below is not empty: it holds four spaces, which is
; what an editor that indents for you leaves behind when you press return
; after the last posting. It ends the entry just as an empty line does, and
; it is written back with its spaces, so saving the file does not change a
; line nobody touched.

2024-03-17 Car wash
    Expenses:Car           $15.00
    Assets:Checking
    
; ── Comment blocks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; A `comment` line on its own opens a block that runs to `end comment`, or
; to the end of the file. Nothing inside it is read: the entry below is
; parked, not booked, so it touches no balance and appears in no report,
; and the file is written back with the block exactly as it was.

comment
Draft of the March rent, not yet due. Everything in here is text.
2024-03-01 * Rent for March
    Expenses:Rent          $1200.00
    Assets:Checking
end comment

% Lines starting with % or | are full-line comments too, as in ledger
| and hledger. So is a line starting with *, the org-mode heading.

; ── A comment after one space on the entry line ─────────────────────────────
; On the line that opens an entry, the description ends at the first `;`,
; however few spaces come before it, and the rest of the line is the entry's
; comment, as hledger reads it. So the payee below is "Parking", and the note
; is kept beside it rather than in it. A `;` inside the (code) belongs to the
; code, which is read first. On a posting line two spaces still end the
; account name.

2024-03-18 (P;7) Parking ; paid at the machine
    Expenses:Car            $4.00
    Assets:Checking

; ── A commodity symbol in quotes ────────────────────────────────────────────
; A symbol that holds a space, a digit or punctuation is written in double
; quotes, as hledger writes it, and the quotes are no part of the name: the
; fund below is ten units of a commodity called Vanguard 500, priced in
; dollars. An entry SwiftLedger rebuilds in that commodity gets the quotes
; back, since the bare name would not read, and a symbol that needs none,
; such as VTI above, is written bare. A commodity line names such a symbol in
; quotes too: bare, the point in BRK.B would read as a decimal mark, and the
; line is refused, here and in hledger.

2024-03-20 Bought into an index fund
    Assets:Funds           10 "Vanguard 500" @ $50.00
    Assets:Checking        -$500.00

commodity "BRK.B"

; ── A default commodity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; A D line names the commodity a bare number is in, from the line it stands
; on, with the side and the style its sample shows, as hledger reads it: the
; 25.00 below is $25.00, and an entry SwiftLedger rebuilds there writes the
; dollar sign in. Above such a line, or in a file without one, a bare number
; is in no commodity at all and is written back bare. The sample states the
; dollar's style too, as every D line does; this one matches the dollars
; above, so nothing already written changes.

D $1000.00

2024-03-21 Lunch, written without a symbol
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  25.00
    Assets:Checking

; ── The mark in force where an entry stands ─────────────────────────────────
; A decimal-mark line speaks from where it stands to the next one, so a file
; can switch marks part way through, as this one does for the refund below,
; copied from a statement that writes euros with a point. An entry
; SwiftLedger rebuilds is written with the mark in force where it stands, not
; the last one the file declares: the refund comes back €4.50 under its
; decimal-mark . line, where €4,50 would read as four hundred and fifty, here
; and in hledger. The commodity line above still speaks for every euro entry
; between it and the switch, and the last line puts the comma back.

decimal-mark .

2024-03-22 Refund for the coffee in Vienna
    Assets:EuroAccount     €4.50
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants

decimal-mark ,
